Real code:

// VendingMachine - the only three things the outside world can trigger
public void selectSlot(String slotId) { state.selectSlot(this, slotId); }
public void insertPayment(double amount) { state.insertPayment(this, amount); }
public void cancel() { state.cancel(this); }

These three methods are the machine's entire public trigger surface, and none of them contains a single if/else about what the machine is currently doing. Where did that decision-making go?,It was removed - the machine now allows every operation at any time,"Into the state object held in the 'state' field: whichever IMachineState is currently active decides what each of these three calls means, including whether it is allowed at all","Into the VendingMachineFacade, which checks the condition before calling these","Into the Slot class, which knows whether it can be selected",,2,"Each condition the machine can be in is a separate class implementing IMachineState. Swapping the object in the 'state' field swaps the behaviour of all three triggers at once, which is why no branching is needed here.",2

Real code:

public class AwaitingPaymentState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("a selection is already in progress, cancel first");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        machine.getPendingPurchase().addAmountReceived(amount);
        System.out.println("received " + amount + ", total so far: "
                + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("cancelling, refunding " + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
    }
}

A customer has already selected slot A1, so the machine is currently in AwaitingPaymentState. Before paying, they now call facade.selectProduct("A2"). What happens?,The selection quietly switches to A2 and the machine keeps waiting for payment,"InvalidMachineStateException is thrown with the message 'a selection is already in progress, cancel first' - the second selection is refused outright",A2 is queued and dispensed after A1 is paid for,The machine returns to IdleState and both selections are discarded,,2,"AwaitingPaymentState refuses selectSlot entirely. The customer must call cancel() first, which resets the pending purchase and returns the machine to IdleState.",1

Real code:

public class IdleState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        Slot slot = machine.findSlot(slotId);
        if (slot == null || !slot.hasStock()) {
            throw new SlotUnavailableException("slot " + slotId + " is not available");
        }
        machine.startPurchase(slot);
        machine.setState(AwaitingPaymentState.getInstance());
        System.out.println("selected " + slot.getProductName() + ", price: " + slot.getPrice());
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("select a product before inserting payment");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is idle");
    }
}

IdleState.insertPayment() throws instead of just holding the money as credit until a product is chosen. Looking at what IdleState.selectSlot() does before payment is possible, why is throwing the reasonable choice here?,Because Java requires interface methods to throw if they are not implemented,"Because the pending purchase object only gets created by startPurchase() during selectSlot - in IdleState there is literally nowhere to record the money against, so accepting it would mean silently losing it",Because coins cannot be physically accepted until a slot is chosen,Because IdleState is a singleton and cannot hold any data,,2,"machine.startPurchase(slot) is what creates the PendingPurchase. Until that has happened, machine.getPendingPurchase() is null, so there is no place to add the money. Throwing makes that impossible situation visible instead of losing the customer's money.",2

Real code:

// From the PARKING LOT project (class-29)
public class AvailableState implements IState {

    @Override
    public void assign(ISpot spot) {
        System.out.println("Assigned available spot");
        spot.setState(OccupiedState.getInstance());
    }

    @Override
    public void release(ISpot spot) {
        System.out.println("cant release , is already available");
    }
}

// From the PARKING LOT project (class-29)
public double unPark(Ticket ticket) {
    ticket.setExitTime(System.currentTimeMillis());
    IPricingStrategy strategy = pricingStrategyFactory.getPricingStrategy(ticket.getVehicle());
    double fee = strategy.calculateFee(ticket);

    ticket.getSpot().release();
    return fee;
}

This is the parking lot from class-29, not the vending machine. Note that unPark() calculates the fee BEFORE calling release(), and that releasing an already-available spot only prints a message. If unPark(ticket) is accidentally called twice with the same ticket, what happens on the second call?,"The second call throws an exception, so the caller knows it failed","The second call recalculates and returns a fee again - and the caller has no way to tell it was a repeat, so the customer is charged twice",The second call returns 0.0 because the spot is already free,The second call is ignored entirely and returns null,,2,"release() on an already-available spot prints 'cant release , is already available' and returns normally. Because unPark() computes the fee before that call and never checks the outcome, the second call looks exactly like a successful first one.",2

Real code:

// From the PARKING LOT project (class-29)
public class AvailableState implements IState {

    @Override
    public void assign(ISpot spot) {
        System.out.println("Assigned available spot");
        spot.setState(OccupiedState.getInstance());
    }

    @Override
    public void release(ISpot spot) {
        System.out.println("cant release , is already available");
    }
}

Continuing from the double-charge problem in the parking lot: what single change to AvailableState.release() would have made that bug impossible, and how does the vending machine already do this?,Return a boolean and have every caller check it,"Throw an exception instead of printing - which is exactly what the vending machine's states do, e.g. AwaitingPaymentState.selectSlot() throws InvalidMachineStateException rather than printing and carrying on",Add a synchronized keyword to release(),Make AvailableState non-singleton so each spot gets its own,,2,This is the direct lesson carried from the parking lot into the vending machine. An operation that is illegal for the current condition must fail loudly

