public class IdleState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        Slot slot = machine.findSlot(slotId);
        if (slot == null || !slot.hasStock()) {
            throw new SlotUnavailableException("slot " + slotId + " is not available");
        }
        machine.startPurchase(slot);
        machine.setState(AwaitingPaymentState.getInstance());
        System.out.println("selected " + slot.getProductName() + ", price: " + slot.getPrice());
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("select a product before inserting payment");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is idle");
    }
}

public class UnavailableState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("machine is unavailable (out of stock)");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("machine is unavailable (out of stock)");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is unavailable");
    }
}

Compare cancel() in these two states with the vending machine's DispensingState, whose cancel() throws InvalidMachineStateException("cannot cancel while dispensing"). Is it inconsistent that some cancels print and one throws?,"Yes - all three should throw, printing is always wrong","No. Cancelling when there is nothing in progress is harmless and the caller's intent (end up with nothing pending) is already satisfied, whereas cancelling mid-dispense is a request the machine genuinely cannot honour","Yes - all three should print, throwing from cancel is always wrong","No, because cancel() is not part of IMachineState",,2,"The useful rule is not 'always throw' but 'never silently fail'. A cancel with nothing pending succeeds trivially. A cancel during dispensing cannot be carried out, so it must be reported.",2

Real code:

public class IdleState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        Slot slot = machine.findSlot(slotId);
        if (slot == null || !slot.hasStock()) {
            throw new SlotUnavailableException("slot " + slotId + " is not available");
        }
        machine.startPurchase(slot);
        machine.setState(AwaitingPaymentState.getInstance());
        System.out.println("selected " + slot.getProductName() + ", price: " + slot.getPrice());
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("select a product before inserting payment");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is idle");
    }
}

IdleState, AwaitingPaymentState, DispensingState and UnavailableState are all singletons with private constructors, and the machine shares one instance of each. What makes that safe here?,Singletons are always safe for state classes,"None of these classes declares any fields - every piece of data they touch arrives as the VendingMachine parameter, so there is nothing per-machine for them to hold",It is safe because there is only ever one VendingMachine,The private constructor prevents the state from being modified,,2,"The state classes are pure behaviour. If a state ever needed to remember something specific to one machine (a retry counter, a timestamp), sharing one instance would immediately break and each machine would need its own.",2

Real code:

public class AwaitingPaymentState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("a selection is already in progress, cancel first");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        machine.getPendingPurchase().addAmountReceived(amount);
        System.out.println("received " + amount + ", total so far: "
                + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("cancelling, refunding " + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
    }
}

The customer has selected the 1.75 Soda and inserted 1.00, so a pending purchase exists with 1.00 received. They now call facade.cancel(). What is the machine's condition afterwards, and what happens to the pending purchase?,"Still AwaitingPaymentState, pending purchase kept with 1.00 recorded","IdleState, and the pending purchase is discarded by resetTransaction() so nothing from the abandoned attempt carries into the next customer's","UnavailableState, because the purchase failed","IdleState, but the pending purchase is kept so the customer can resume later",,2,resetTransaction() sets pendingPurchase back to null and the state goes back to Idle. Leaving stale purchase data behind is exactly how the next customer would end up with someone else's credit.,1

Real code:

private Transaction completePurchase(PaymentMethod method) {
    Slot slot = machine.getPendingPurchase().getSlot();
    double amountReceived = machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived();
    double change = amountReceived - slot.getPrice();

    if (!machine.canMakeChange(change)) {
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
        throw new ChangeUnavailableException("cannot make change of " + change
                + ", refunding " + amountReceived);
    }

    machine.setState(DispensingState.getInstance());

    slot.dispenseOne();

    IPaymentStrategy strategy = paymentStrategyFactory.getStrategy(method);

    if (strategy.doesAffectChangeReserve()) {
        machine.releaseChange(change);
        machine.receiveCash(amountReceived);
    }

    Transaction transaction = machine.getPendingPurchase()
            .complete(change, method, System.currentTimeMillis());
    transactionManager.save(transaction);

    machine.resetTransaction();
    machine.setState(machine.hasAnyStock() ? IdleState.getInstance() : UnavailableState.getInstance());

    return transaction;
}

This method belongs to VendingMachineFacade, not to any state class, yet it calls machine.setState(...) twice directly. Given that state transitions elsewhere happen inside the state classes themselves, what is the honest assessment of these two lines?,It is correct and required - facades are always responsible for transitions,"It is a leak: the facade is now a second place that drives transitions, so to know how the machine moves between conditions you must read both the state classes and this method","It has no effect, because setState is ignored during a purchase",It is required because DispensingState has no way to transition out of itself,,2,"Worth arguing about in class. It works, and DispensingState genuinely has no trigger that would move it along - but the cost is that the transition rules are no longer in one place, which was a main reason for using state objects at all.",2

