public class UnavailableState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("machine is unavailable (out of stock)");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("machine is unavailable (out of stock)");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is unavailable");
    }
}

// VendingMachineFacade, last line of completePurchase()
machine.setState(machine.hasAnyStock() ? IdleState.getInstance() : UnavailableState.getInstance());

// Slot.java
public void restock(int units) {
    quantity += units;
}

Searching the whole project, this ternary is the ONLY place UnavailableState is ever entered, and nothing anywhere calls restock(). Once the last item is sold, can the machine ever serve a customer again?,Yes - calling restock() on a slot automatically returns the machine to IdleState,"No. Nothing transitions out of UnavailableState, and UnavailableState.selectSlot() throws, so the machine is permanently bricked even if a technician refills a slot",Yes - the machine returns to IdleState on the next selectSlot attempt,Yes - UnavailableState.cancel() moves it back to Idle,,2,"A real gap, not a trick question. restock() exists but is never wired to a state transition, and no operation is legal in UnavailableState. A working design needs a restock trigger that puts the machine back into Idle.",2

Real code:

public class IdleState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        Slot slot = machine.findSlot(slotId);
        if (slot == null || !slot.hasStock()) {
            throw new SlotUnavailableException("slot " + slotId + " is not available");
        }
        machine.startPurchase(slot);
        machine.setState(AwaitingPaymentState.getInstance());
        System.out.println("selected " + slot.getProductName() + ", price: " + slot.getPrice());
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("select a product before inserting payment");
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("nothing to cancel, machine is idle");
    }
}

IdleState.selectSlot() throws SlotUnavailableException for an empty slot, while its insertPayment() throws InvalidMachineStateException. Why use two different exception types rather than one?,Purely arbitrary - one exception type would be equally clear,"They describe different failures: 'you asked for something that is out of stock' is about the slot, while 'that operation is not legal right now' is about the machine's condition. A caller may reasonably want to handle those differently",SlotUnavailableException is required because Slot is in a different package,InvalidMachineStateException cannot be thrown from selectSlot,,2,"Main demonstrates the difference: it catches SlotUnavailableException to report a sold-out product, which is a normal customer-facing outcome, not a programming error.",1

Real code:

public interface IMachineState {
    void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId);
    void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount);
    void cancel(VendingMachine machine);
}

You are asked to add a MaintenanceState in which nothing may be selected or paid for, entered when a technician opens the machine. Which of the following are needed? (select all that apply),A new MaintenanceState class implementing IMachineState,Some trigger that calls machine.setState(MaintenanceState.getInstance()),"Edits to IdleState, AwaitingPaymentState, DispensingState and UnavailableState so they know about the new condition",A new method added to the IMachineState interface,An if/else added to VendingMachine.selectSlot() for the maintenance case,"1,2","The four existing states, the interface and the VendingMachine trigger methods are all untouched. Only the new class plus a way to enter it are required - that is the whole payoff of putting each condition in its own class.",2

Real code:

// VendingMachine - the only three things the outside world can trigger
public void selectSlot(String slotId) { state.selectSlot(this, slotId); }
public void insertPayment(double amount) { state.insertPayment(this, amount); }
public void cancel() { state.cancel(this); }

Suppose the four state classes were deleted and replaced with an enum field plus a switch inside each of these three methods. The program's output today would be identical. What specifically gets worse?,Nothing measurable - with four conditions the switch is arguably easier to follow in one place,"Adding a fifth condition means editing three switches that already work correctly, and the rules for one condition are scattered across three methods instead of sitting together in one class","It would not compile, because you cannot switch on an enum in Java",The machine would no longer be able to change conditions at runtime,,2,"The honest trade-off: at four conditions, one switch really is readable. The cost lands on the fifth condition, and on the day you want to read everything Idle does without hunting through three separate methods.",2

Real code:

public class AwaitingPaymentState implements IMachineState {

    @Override
    public void selectSlot(VendingMachine machine, String slotId) {
        throw new InvalidMachineStateException("a selection is already in progress, cancel first");
    }

    @Override
    public void insertPayment(VendingMachine machine, double amount) {
        machine.getPendingPurchase().addAmountReceived(amount);
        System.out.println("received " + amount + ", total so far: "
                + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
    }

    @Override
    public void cancel(VendingMachine machine) {
        System.out.println("cancelling, refunding " + machine.getPendingPurchase().getAmountReceived());
        machine.resetTransaction();
        machine.setState(IdleState.getInstance());
    }
}

Note the comment the class carries above cancel(): 'cancel should return the amount that has been paid so far instead of just printing it' and 'no money movement has happened'. A customer inserts 1.00 in cash and then cancels. What actually happens to that coin in the current code?,It is returned to the customer by ChangeService,"Nothing moves. The message says 'refunding 1.0' but no code touches the change reserve or returns anything to the caller, so the coin's fate is simply unrecorded",It is added to the change reserve as machine income,It is carried over as credit for the next customer,,2,The printed message describes an intention the code does not carry out. This is the same failure shape as the parking lot's release(): a message that reads like success while nothing happened.,2
